1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,791
There is only one thing in the entire

2
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:05,381
universe you can be absolutely sure

3
00:00:05,381 --> 00:00:06,029
exists.

4
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:08,752
Not the chair you're sitting on.

5
00:00:09,402 --> 00:00:11,331
Not the people you love.

6
00:00:11,981 --> 00:00:14,554
Not even the screen in front of you.

7
00:00:15,204 --> 00:00:17,675
Just one thing: your own mind.

8
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:21,542
Everything else — every face, every voice,

9
00:00:21,542 --> 00:00:24,616
every memory of a world outside — could,

10
00:00:24,616 --> 00:00:27,619
in principle, be something your mind is

11
00:00:27,619 --> 00:00:29,335
producing on its own.

12
00:00:29,985 --> 00:00:32,781
This idea is called solipsism.

13
00:00:33,431 --> 00:00:35,800
And here's what makes it unsettling:

14
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,473
for more than two thousand years, some of

15
00:00:38,473 --> 00:00:40,903
the greatest thinkers in history have

16
00:00:40,903 --> 00:00:42,665
tried to prove it wrong...

17
00:00:43,315 --> 00:00:45,773
...and they still argue about whether

18
00:00:45,773 --> 00:00:47,247
anyone has succeeded.

19
00:00:47,897 --> 00:00:50,220
By the end of this video, you'll know

20
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,904
where this idea came from,

21
00:00:52,554 --> 00:00:54,794
why your brain spent years of your

22
00:00:54,794 --> 00:00:56,913
childhood learning to escape it,

23
00:00:57,563 --> 00:01:00,150
and why one famous logician openly

24
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:02,247
admitted she believed it.

25
00:01:02,897 --> 00:01:04,990
Quick favor before we start:

26
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,960
if you like ideas that break your brain a

27
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:09,513
little, subscribe.

28
00:01:09,513 --> 00:01:11,808
It genuinely helps the channel.

29
00:01:12,458 --> 00:01:14,302
The word comes from Latin:

30
00:01:14,302 --> 00:01:16,844
solus, meaning "alone," and ipse,

31
00:01:16,844 --> 00:01:19,323
meaning "self." Alone with the self.

32
00:01:19,973 --> 00:01:22,510
Philosophers usually separate two

33
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:23,356
versions.

34
00:01:24,006 --> 00:01:26,769
The strong version, metaphysical

35
00:01:26,769 --> 00:01:28,304
solipsism, says:

36
00:01:28,304 --> 00:01:30,070
only my mind exists.

37
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:33,447
That's it. The world and other people are

38
00:01:33,447 --> 00:01:36,057
just contents of my experience.

39
00:01:36,707 --> 00:01:39,534
The weaker version, epistemological

40
00:01:39,534 --> 00:01:42,434
solipsism, is more modest — and much

41
00:01:42,434 --> 00:01:43,883
harder to escape.

42
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:47,656
It says: I can't know that anything

43
00:01:47,656 --> 00:01:49,686
outside my mind exists.

44
00:01:49,686 --> 00:01:52,809
Maybe it does. I just can't prove it.

45
00:01:53,459 --> 00:01:56,253
Most philosophers reject the first.

46
00:01:56,253 --> 00:01:59,194
The second is the one that keeps them

47
00:01:59,194 --> 00:02:00,517
awake at night.

48
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:04,185
The idea is older than you might think.

49
00:02:04,835 --> 00:02:07,568
Around the fifth century BC, the Greek

50
00:02:07,568 --> 00:02:10,302
thinker Gorgias wrote a work usually

51
00:02:10,302 --> 00:02:11,968
called On Non-Being.

52
00:02:12,618 --> 00:02:14,971
Its argument came in three steps:

53
00:02:14,971 --> 00:02:17,912
nothing exists; even if something existed,

54
00:02:17,912 --> 00:02:20,853
it couldn't be known; and even if it could

55
00:02:20,853 --> 00:02:23,663
be known, it couldn't be communicated to

56
00:02:23,663 --> 00:02:24,643
anyone else.

57
00:02:25,293 --> 00:02:28,116
Whether Gorgias meant it seriously or as a

58
00:02:28,116 --> 00:02:30,750
showpiece of clever rhetoric, the third

59
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:33,196
step points straight at our problem:

60
00:02:33,196 --> 00:02:35,830
your experience is locked inside you.

61
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:38,918
In ancient China, the philosopher

62
00:02:38,918 --> 00:02:41,220
Zhuangzi told a famous story.

63
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:44,882
He dreamed he was a butterfly, fluttering

64
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:47,689
happily, with no idea he was Zhuangzi.

65
00:02:48,339 --> 00:02:51,690
Then he woke up — and wasn't sure anymore.

66
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:55,536
Was he a man who had dreamed of being a

67
00:02:55,536 --> 00:02:58,807
butterfly... or a butterfly now dreaming

68
00:02:58,807 --> 00:03:00,024
he was a man?

69
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:03,551
Meanwhile, in India, a similar question

70
00:03:03,551 --> 00:03:05,264
took a different path.

71
00:03:05,914 --> 00:03:08,694
Around the fourth or fifth century, the

72
00:03:08,694 --> 00:03:11,606
Buddhist philosopher Vasubandhu wrote a

73
00:03:11,606 --> 00:03:14,452
short text known as the Twenty Verses.

74
00:03:15,102 --> 00:03:17,983
His school, Yogācāra, is often called

75
00:03:17,983 --> 00:03:19,629
"consciousness-only."

76
00:03:20,279 --> 00:03:22,835
His starting point sounds familiar:

77
00:03:22,835 --> 00:03:25,659
in a dream, you see places, people, and

78
00:03:25,659 --> 00:03:28,484
objects that feel completely real — yet

79
00:03:28,484 --> 00:03:30,905
there's nothing outside your mind

80
00:03:30,905 --> 00:03:32,116
producing them.

81
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:35,028
So how do we know waking life is

82
00:03:35,028 --> 00:03:35,868
different?

83
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:38,226
But here's the twist:

84
00:03:38,226 --> 00:03:40,788
Vasubandhu was not a solipsist.

85
00:03:41,438 --> 00:03:44,643
His tradition accepted many minds — many

86
00:03:44,643 --> 00:03:47,923
streams of consciousness, influencing one

87
00:03:47,923 --> 00:03:48,743
another.

88
00:03:49,393 --> 00:03:52,238
The question wasn't "Am I the only one?"

89
00:03:52,238 --> 00:03:55,017
It was "What are we really experiencing

90
00:03:55,017 --> 00:03:57,068
when we experience a world?"

91
00:03:57,718 --> 00:04:00,467
Centuries later, the Hindu philosopher

92
00:04:00,467 --> 00:04:03,283
Adi Shankara taught that the everyday

93
00:04:03,283 --> 00:04:05,898
world of separate things is maya —

94
00:04:05,898 --> 00:04:06,904
appearance —

95
00:04:07,554 --> 00:04:10,342
and that, at the deepest level, there is

96
00:04:10,342 --> 00:04:11,444
only one Self.

97
00:04:12,094 --> 00:04:15,028
That's not "only my mind exists" either.

98
00:04:15,028 --> 00:04:18,030
It's closer to: the line between "me" and

99
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:20,760
"everything else" may not be where we

100
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:21,374
think.

101
00:04:22,024 --> 00:04:25,672
Two thousand years later, in the 1600s, a

102
00:04:25,672 --> 00:04:29,237
French philosopher decided to take doubt

103
00:04:29,237 --> 00:04:31,309
to its absolute limit.

104
00:04:31,959 --> 00:04:34,707
His name was René Descartes.

105
00:04:35,357 --> 00:04:37,766
His plan was simple and radical:

106
00:04:37,766 --> 00:04:40,863
throw out every belief that could possibly

107
00:04:40,863 --> 00:04:43,753
be false, and see if anything survives.

108
00:04:44,403 --> 00:04:46,064
First, the senses.

109
00:04:46,064 --> 00:04:49,305
They fool us all the time — a straight

110
00:04:49,305 --> 00:04:51,598
stick looks bent in water.

111
00:04:52,248 --> 00:04:54,494
So they can't be fully trusted.

112
00:04:55,144 --> 00:04:57,209
Then, the dream argument.

113
00:04:57,209 --> 00:05:00,101
Every night you experience vivid worlds

114
00:05:00,101 --> 00:05:03,131
that feel completely real while you're in

115
00:05:03,131 --> 00:05:03,682
them.

116
00:05:04,332 --> 00:05:06,962
Can you be certain, right now, that you're

117
00:05:06,962 --> 00:05:07,488
awake?

118
00:05:08,138 --> 00:05:10,639
And finally, his most famous thought

119
00:05:10,639 --> 00:05:11,537
experiment.

120
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:14,660
What if there were an evil demon,

121
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:17,523
supremely powerful and cunning, using all

122
00:05:17,523 --> 00:05:19,475
its energy to deceive you —

123
00:05:20,125 --> 00:05:22,973
feeding you a fake world, fake people,

124
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:24,710
even fake mathematics?

125
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,337
Descartes pushed until almost nothing was

126
00:05:28,337 --> 00:05:28,878
left.

127
00:05:29,528 --> 00:05:32,297
And then he found the one thing the demon

128
00:05:32,297 --> 00:05:33,555
could never fake.

129
00:05:34,205 --> 00:05:37,210
If you are being deceived, then you exist

130
00:05:37,210 --> 00:05:40,078
— someone must be there to be deceived.

131
00:05:40,728 --> 00:05:43,044
Doubting is a kind of thinking, and

132
00:05:43,044 --> 00:05:44,933
thinking requires a thinker.

133
00:05:45,583 --> 00:05:48,658
In his words: I think, therefore I am.

134
00:05:49,308 --> 00:05:52,146
It's one of the most famous sentences in

135
00:05:52,146 --> 00:05:53,070
philosophy.

136
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,550
But notice what it actually proves.

137
00:05:56,550 --> 00:05:58,785
It proves that you exist.

138
00:05:59,435 --> 00:06:02,426
Not your body — just your thinking.

139
00:06:03,076 --> 00:06:05,848
And it says absolutely nothing about

140
00:06:05,848 --> 00:06:06,914
anyone else.

141
00:06:07,564 --> 00:06:10,395
Descartes had dug himself into a hole, and

142
00:06:10,395 --> 00:06:11,275
he knew it.

143
00:06:11,925 --> 00:06:14,906
To climb out, he argued that a perfect God

144
00:06:14,906 --> 00:06:17,423
exists and would not allow us to be

145
00:06:17,423 --> 00:06:19,344
deceived about everything.

146
00:06:19,994 --> 00:06:22,682
Many later readers found that step much

147
00:06:22,682 --> 00:06:25,562
weaker than the doubt that came before it.

148
00:06:26,212 --> 00:06:29,041
The hole he dug was convincing.

149
00:06:29,041 --> 00:06:31,371
The ladder out was not.

150
00:06:32,021 --> 00:06:34,841
A century later, the Irish bishop George

151
00:06:34,841 --> 00:06:36,828
Berkeley went even further.

152
00:06:37,478 --> 00:06:41,060
In 1710 he argued that physical objects

153
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:44,386
are nothing more than collections of

154
00:06:44,386 --> 00:06:45,666
perceptions.

155
00:06:46,316 --> 00:06:49,129
To be, he said, is to be perceived.

156
00:06:49,779 --> 00:06:53,113
Berkeley wasn't a solipsist — he believed

157
00:06:53,113 --> 00:06:55,614
other minds and God were real.

158
00:06:56,264 --> 00:06:58,717
But critics said his ideas slid

159
00:06:58,717 --> 00:07:00,232
dangerously close.

160
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:03,413
His most famous critic didn't use an

161
00:07:03,413 --> 00:07:04,646
argument at all.

162
00:07:05,296 --> 00:07:08,830
In 1763, the writer Samuel Johnson was

163
00:07:08,830 --> 00:07:12,529
walking with his biographer James Boswell,

164
00:07:12,529 --> 00:07:15,981
who remarked that Berkeley's theory was

165
00:07:15,981 --> 00:07:17,954
impossible to refute.

166
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:21,399
Johnson walked up to a large stone, kicked

167
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:24,070
it hard enough to bounce back, and said:

168
00:07:24,070 --> 00:07:25,561
"I refute it thus."

169
00:07:26,211 --> 00:07:27,884
It's a great moment.

170
00:07:27,884 --> 00:07:31,158
It's also, philosophers will tell you, not

171
00:07:31,158 --> 00:07:32,831
a refutation at all.

172
00:07:33,481 --> 00:07:36,589
Berkeley never denied that you feel the

173
00:07:36,589 --> 00:07:39,768
stone. He only questioned what the feeling

174
00:07:39,768 --> 00:07:40,757
is made of.

175
00:07:41,407 --> 00:07:44,232
And this brings us to the heart of it — a

176
00:07:44,232 --> 00:07:46,993
puzzle philosophers call the problem of

177
00:07:46,993 --> 00:07:48,020
other minds.

178
00:07:48,670 --> 00:07:51,219
You see other people laugh, cry, flinch

179
00:07:51,219 --> 00:07:52,494
when they're hurt.

180
00:07:53,144 --> 00:07:56,024
But you never experience their pain.

181
00:07:56,024 --> 00:07:58,092
You only see the outside:

182
00:07:58,092 --> 00:08:00,381
a face, a sound, a movement.

183
00:08:01,031 --> 00:08:03,582
Their inner world, if there is one, is

184
00:08:03,582 --> 00:08:05,511
permanently hidden from you.

185
00:08:06,161 --> 00:08:09,952
In 1865, John Stuart Mill offered the

186
00:08:09,952 --> 00:08:13,291
classic answer: the argument from

187
00:08:13,291 --> 00:08:14,374
analogy.

188
00:08:15,024 --> 00:08:17,062
When I'm hurt, I wince.

189
00:08:17,062 --> 00:08:20,118
Others wince in the same situations.

190
00:08:20,118 --> 00:08:23,566
So, by analogy, they probably feel what I

191
00:08:23,566 --> 00:08:24,193
feel.

192
00:08:24,843 --> 00:08:28,054
It sounds reasonable — until you notice

193
00:08:28,054 --> 00:08:31,112
the problem. You're generalizing from

194
00:08:31,112 --> 00:08:33,023
exactly one example:

195
00:08:33,023 --> 00:08:33,941
yourself.

196
00:08:34,591 --> 00:08:37,533
Modern philosophers have made the puzzle

197
00:08:37,533 --> 00:08:38,628
even sharper.

198
00:08:39,278 --> 00:08:43,140
In 1996, David Chalmers popularized the

199
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:46,299
idea of a philosophical zombie:

200
00:08:46,949 --> 00:08:49,933
a being that looks and acts exactly like a

201
00:08:49,933 --> 00:08:52,850
person, says "ouch," writes poetry, falls

202
00:08:52,850 --> 00:08:53,646
in love —

203
00:08:54,296 --> 00:08:56,727
but has no inner experience at all.

204
00:08:57,377 --> 00:09:00,171
The lights are on, but nobody's home.

205
00:09:00,821 --> 00:09:03,008
Here's the uncomfortable question:

206
00:09:03,008 --> 00:09:05,669
from the outside, how would you ever tell

207
00:09:05,669 --> 00:09:06,792
the difference?

208
00:09:07,442 --> 00:09:09,256
Let's try something.

209
00:09:09,256 --> 00:09:12,805
Think of someone you know well — a friend,

210
00:09:12,805 --> 00:09:13,751
a parent.

211
00:09:14,401 --> 00:09:17,646
Now try to prove — not believe, but

212
00:09:17,646 --> 00:09:21,053
prove — that they feel anything at all.

213
00:09:21,703 --> 00:09:24,948
You'll notice that every piece of evidence

214
00:09:24,948 --> 00:09:27,833
is behavior: words, faces, reactions.

215
00:09:28,483 --> 00:09:31,549
Now flip it. How would they prove that

216
00:09:31,549 --> 00:09:33,189
you feel anything?

217
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:36,800
You are certain about your own pain, and

218
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:39,211
uncertain about everyone else's.

219
00:09:39,211 --> 00:09:42,173
That asymmetry is the whole problem in a

220
00:09:42,173 --> 00:09:43,344
single breath.

221
00:09:43,994 --> 00:09:46,436
There's even a word for the opposite

222
00:09:46,436 --> 00:09:49,066
feeling: sonder, coined by the writer

223
00:09:49,066 --> 00:09:50,005
John Koenig.

224
00:09:50,655 --> 00:09:53,632
It's the sudden realization that every

225
00:09:53,632 --> 00:09:56,682
stranger around you has a life as vivid

226
00:09:56,682 --> 00:09:58,933
and complicated as your own.

227
00:09:59,583 --> 00:10:02,423
And here's where psychology gives us a

228
00:10:02,423 --> 00:10:03,808
surprising twist.

229
00:10:03,808 --> 00:10:06,925
In a sense, every one of us starts life as

230
00:10:06,925 --> 00:10:08,518
a kind of solipsist.

231
00:10:09,168 --> 00:10:12,012
Very young children struggle to understand

232
00:10:12,012 --> 00:10:14,730
that other people have beliefs different

233
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:15,868
from their own.

234
00:10:16,518 --> 00:10:19,289
Psychologists test this with what's called

235
00:10:19,289 --> 00:10:20,828
a false-belief task.

236
00:10:21,478 --> 00:10:24,556
In one well-known version, the Sally–Anne

237
00:10:24,556 --> 00:10:27,566
test, a child watches a doll named Sally

238
00:10:27,566 --> 00:10:30,165
put a marble in a basket and leave.

239
00:10:30,815 --> 00:10:33,592
While she's gone, Anne moves the marble

240
00:10:33,592 --> 00:10:34,518
into a box.

241
00:10:35,168 --> 00:10:36,731
Then the child is asked:

242
00:10:36,731 --> 00:10:39,048
when Sally comes back, where will she

243
00:10:39,048 --> 00:10:39,511
look?

244
00:10:40,161 --> 00:10:41,998
Most three-year-olds say:

245
00:10:41,998 --> 00:10:44,950
the box — because that's where they know

246
00:10:44,950 --> 00:10:45,540
it is.

247
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:49,096
Around age four, most children start to

248
00:10:49,096 --> 00:10:50,617
answer: the basket.

249
00:10:51,267 --> 00:10:53,517
They've realized something huge:

250
00:10:53,517 --> 00:10:56,410
other people have minds of their own, with

251
00:10:56,410 --> 00:10:59,302
their own, sometimes wrong, picture of the

252
00:10:59,302 --> 00:10:59,881
world.

253
00:11:00,530 --> 00:11:03,640
Psychologists call this ability theory of

254
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:04,262
mind.

255
00:11:04,912 --> 00:11:07,867
So in a way, your brain already made its

256
00:11:07,867 --> 00:11:10,891
leap out of solipsism — not by proof, but

257
00:11:10,891 --> 00:11:12,266
by development.

258
00:11:12,916 --> 00:11:15,879
And sometimes, that sense of reality can

259
00:11:15,879 --> 00:11:16,430
slip.

260
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:20,036
Many people experience brief moments of

261
00:11:20,036 --> 00:11:23,203
derealization — a feeling that the world

262
00:11:23,203 --> 00:11:26,089
is flat, distant, dreamlike, not quite

263
00:11:26,089 --> 00:11:26,652
real.

264
00:11:27,302 --> 00:11:30,439
When it's persistent and distressing, it's

265
00:11:30,439 --> 00:11:33,087
recognized as a clinical condition,

266
00:11:33,087 --> 00:11:35,526
depersonalization-derealization

267
00:11:35,526 --> 00:11:38,593
disorder, estimated to affect around two

268
00:11:38,593 --> 00:11:40,057
percent of people.

269
00:11:40,707 --> 00:11:43,950
In the 2000s, psychiatrist Joel Gold and

270
00:11:43,950 --> 00:11:47,050
his brother, the philosopher Ian Gold,

271
00:11:47,050 --> 00:11:50,005
began describing patients who believed

272
00:11:50,005 --> 00:11:52,960
their entire life was a staged reality

273
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:56,204
show, with everyone around them as actors.

274
00:11:56,854 --> 00:11:59,800
They called it the Truman Show delusion.

275
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:03,141
The philosophers' thought experiment, it

276
00:12:03,141 --> 00:12:05,645
turns out, can become someone's lived

277
00:12:05,645 --> 00:12:06,522
experience.

278
00:12:07,172 --> 00:12:09,916
Now, the logician I promised you.

279
00:12:10,566 --> 00:12:13,717
Christine Ladd-Franklin was a brilliant

280
00:12:13,717 --> 00:12:16,438
American logician and psychologist.

281
00:12:17,088 --> 00:12:19,846
In his 1948 book Human Knowledge,

282
00:12:19,846 --> 00:12:22,895
Bertrand Russell recalled receiving a

283
00:12:22,895 --> 00:12:24,274
letter from her.

284
00:12:24,924 --> 00:12:27,462
She told him she was a solipsist — and

285
00:12:27,462 --> 00:12:29,939
that she was surprised there were no

286
00:12:29,939 --> 00:12:30,620
others.

287
00:12:31,270 --> 00:12:34,442
Think about that sentence for a second.

288
00:12:34,442 --> 00:12:37,765
If solipsism is true, of course there are

289
00:12:37,765 --> 00:12:40,559
no others. That's the whole point.

290
00:12:41,209 --> 00:12:43,128
And yet she was surprised.

291
00:12:43,778 --> 00:12:46,958
It's funny. But it also shows something

292
00:12:46,958 --> 00:12:50,289
important: solipsism is almost impossible

293
00:12:50,289 --> 00:12:52,257
to live consistently.

294
00:12:52,907 --> 00:12:55,468
The moment you talk, write, argue, or

295
00:12:55,468 --> 00:12:58,156
expect to be understood, you're already

296
00:12:58,156 --> 00:13:00,845
acting as if other minds are out there.

297
00:13:01,495 --> 00:13:03,943
The Austrian philosopher Ludwig

298
00:13:03,943 --> 00:13:07,091
Wittgenstein turned that observation into

299
00:13:07,091 --> 00:13:09,889
one of the most powerful responses to

300
00:13:09,889 --> 00:13:10,799
solipsism.

301
00:13:11,449 --> 00:13:13,908
His point, roughly, was this:

302
00:13:13,908 --> 00:13:16,828
the solipsist expresses the idea in

303
00:13:16,828 --> 00:13:19,748
language. But language is public.

304
00:13:20,398 --> 00:13:23,363
Words get their meaning from shared rules,

305
00:13:23,363 --> 00:13:25,011
from use among people.

306
00:13:25,661 --> 00:13:28,794
A truly private language — one that only

307
00:13:28,794 --> 00:13:31,788
you could ever understand — may not even

308
00:13:31,788 --> 00:13:32,832
be possible.

309
00:13:33,482 --> 00:13:36,613
So the very sentence "only my mind exists"

310
00:13:36,613 --> 00:13:39,605
borrows its meaning from a world full of

311
00:13:39,605 --> 00:13:40,858
other speakers.

312
00:13:41,508 --> 00:13:44,531
Strangely, in his early work, Wittgenstein

313
00:13:44,531 --> 00:13:47,554
also wrote that what the solipsist means

314
00:13:47,554 --> 00:13:50,376
is, in a sense, quite correct — it just

315
00:13:50,376 --> 00:13:51,719
cannot be said.

316
00:13:52,369 --> 00:13:54,387
It can only show itself.

317
00:13:54,387 --> 00:13:57,675
Philosophers are still arguing about what

318
00:13:57,675 --> 00:13:59,170
exactly he meant.

319
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:03,170
Today, the old demon has a new costume.

320
00:14:03,820 --> 00:14:07,503
In 1981, the philosopher Hilary Putnam

321
00:14:07,503 --> 00:14:10,243
described a brain in a vat:

322
00:14:10,893 --> 00:14:14,084
a brain kept alive by scientists, wired to

323
00:14:14,084 --> 00:14:16,850
a computer that feeds it a perfectly

324
00:14:16,850 --> 00:14:18,268
convincing world.

325
00:14:18,918 --> 00:14:21,406
How could you know you're not that brain?

326
00:14:22,056 --> 00:14:24,566
Putnam himself argued the scenario

327
00:14:24,566 --> 00:14:27,348
undermines itself — that if you really

328
00:14:27,348 --> 00:14:30,130
were such a brain, your words couldn't

329
00:14:30,130 --> 00:14:32,708
even refer to real brains and vats.

330
00:14:33,358 --> 00:14:36,205
Not everyone accepts his argument.

331
00:14:36,855 --> 00:14:39,894
And in 2003, Nick Bostrom published the

332
00:14:39,894 --> 00:14:42,726
simulation argument, suggesting that

333
00:14:42,726 --> 00:14:45,558
under certain assumptions, we might be

334
00:14:45,558 --> 00:14:47,976
living in a computer simulation.

335
00:14:48,626 --> 00:14:52,238
That isn't solipsism — a simulation could

336
00:14:52,238 --> 00:14:54,454
contain many real minds.

337
00:14:55,104 --> 00:14:57,679
But it reopens Descartes's question:

338
00:14:57,679 --> 00:15:00,451
how much of what you experience can you

339
00:15:00,451 --> 00:15:01,837
actually verify?

340
00:15:02,487 --> 00:15:05,193
Pop culture loves this question.

341
00:15:05,843 --> 00:15:08,523
In The Truman Show, a man slowly

342
00:15:08,523 --> 00:15:11,781
discovers that his whole life is a TV set,

343
00:15:11,781 --> 00:15:14,461
and everyone he knows is an actor.

344
00:15:15,111 --> 00:15:18,264
In The Matrix, the world is a simulation

345
00:15:18,264 --> 00:15:21,138
fed into people's brains — Descartes's

346
00:15:21,138 --> 00:15:23,240
demon with better graphics.

347
00:15:23,890 --> 00:15:26,453
In Inception, characters carry small

348
00:15:26,453 --> 00:15:29,203
objects to test whether they're dreaming.

349
00:15:29,853 --> 00:15:32,979
None of these stories are really solipsism

350
00:15:32,979 --> 00:15:35,898
— there are always other real people in

351
00:15:35,898 --> 00:15:36,454
them.

352
00:15:37,104 --> 00:15:38,725
Maybe that's telling:

353
00:15:38,725 --> 00:15:41,428
even our wildest fantasies about fake

354
00:15:41,428 --> 00:15:44,401
worlds can't quite let go of other minds.

355
00:15:45,051 --> 00:15:48,024
So why isn't everyone a solipsist?

356
00:15:48,674 --> 00:15:51,223
One answer: inference to the best

357
00:15:51,223 --> 00:15:52,325
explanation.

358
00:15:52,975 --> 00:15:55,820
The world behaves too consistently, too

359
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:58,665
surprisingly, too independently of your

360
00:15:58,665 --> 00:15:59,342
wishes.

361
00:15:59,992 --> 00:16:02,399
Other people tell you things you didn't

362
00:16:02,399 --> 00:16:04,749
know, disagree with you, surprise you.

363
00:16:05,399 --> 00:16:08,172
The simplest explanation is that they're

364
00:16:08,172 --> 00:16:08,817
real.

365
00:16:09,467 --> 00:16:12,309
And another answer comes from the Scottish

366
00:16:12,309 --> 00:16:14,078
philosopher David Hume.

367
00:16:14,728 --> 00:16:17,875
Hume took skeptical doubts seriously — and

368
00:16:17,875 --> 00:16:20,952
admitted they didn't survive contact with

369
00:16:20,952 --> 00:16:22,140
ordinary life.

370
00:16:22,790 --> 00:16:25,374
He wrote that he would dine, play a game

371
00:16:25,374 --> 00:16:27,897
of backgammon, talk and be merry with

372
00:16:27,897 --> 00:16:28,798
his friends,

373
00:16:29,448 --> 00:16:32,224
and after a few hours, his speculations

374
00:16:32,224 --> 00:16:35,001
would seem so cold and strained that he

375
00:16:35,001 --> 00:16:37,910
couldn't bring himself to return to them.

376
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:41,555
Nature, in other words, is stronger than

377
00:16:41,555 --> 00:16:42,575
philosophy.

378
00:16:43,225 --> 00:16:45,314
So here's where we end up.

379
00:16:45,964 --> 00:16:48,959
You cannot prove, with absolute certainty,

380
00:16:48,959 --> 00:16:51,022
that anyone else has a mind.

381
00:16:51,672 --> 00:16:53,206
Not your friends.

382
00:16:53,206 --> 00:16:56,425
Not your family. Not the person reading

383
00:16:56,425 --> 00:16:58,955
the comments under this video.

384
00:16:59,605 --> 00:17:02,424
But every day, you act as if they do.

385
00:17:03,074 --> 00:17:05,754
You apologize. You explain.

386
00:17:05,754 --> 00:17:08,523
You hope to be understood.

387
00:17:09,173 --> 00:17:12,883
Maybe that's the real answer to solipsism.

388
00:17:12,883 --> 00:17:15,520
Not a proof — but a choice.

389
00:17:16,170 --> 00:17:19,182
A choice to treat the minds around you as

390
00:17:19,182 --> 00:17:21,715
real, because the alternative is a

391
00:17:21,715 --> 00:17:24,385
universe with only one person in it.

392
00:17:25,035 --> 00:17:27,685
Because in the end, there is only one

393
00:17:27,685 --> 00:17:30,666
thing you can be absolutely sure exists...

394
00:17:30,666 --> 00:17:33,448
and it's lonely in there by yourself.

